Name: useDesktopAppearance

Type: property

Syntax: set the useDesktopAppearance to {true | false}

Syntax: get the useDesktopAppearance

Summary:
Specifies whether the <systemAppearance> on Linux follows the desktop's
light or dark setting.

Introduced: 9.7.1

OS: mac, windows, linux, html5

Platforms: desktop

Example:
set the useDesktopAppearance to true

Example:
if the platform is "Linux" then set the useDesktopAppearance to true

Value (bool):
The <useDesktopAppearance> is true or false. By default, it is false.

Description:
Use the <useDesktopAppearance> property to let an application on Linux
see the desktop's dark mode in the <systemAppearance>.

On Linux the <systemAppearance> is "light" by default, whatever the
desktop shows, as it always has been. When the <useDesktopAppearance> is
true, it is "dark" while the desktop is set to dark: GNOME's
"color-scheme" setting (GNOME 42 and later, and the desktops that use its
settings) set to "prefer-dark", or, where there is no such setting or it
is "default", a GTK theme whose name has "dark" in it, such as
"Adwaita-dark". The <systemAppearanceChanged> message is then also sent
when the "color-scheme" setting switches the <systemAppearance>, and when
setting the <useDesktopAppearance> changes it.

The <useDesktopAppearance> changes only what the <systemAppearance>
reports: the engine draws controls in the colours of the desktop's GTK
theme either way. It is a global property: it is not saved with a
<stack>, so an application that wants it sets it when it starts, for
example in its <openStack> <handler>. The development environment sets
it from the "Follow the desktop's light or dark setting" setting in the
Appearance pane of its Preferences on Linux.

On macOS and Windows the <systemAppearance> follows the system already,
and the <useDesktopAppearance> changes nothing there.

References: systemAppearance (property),
systemAppearanceChanged (message), openStack (message),
useSystemWindowColor (property)

Tags: ui
